
DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

$a = random(2001, 2040);
$b = random(1,    4);
$c = random(1,    4);

$numer = Compute("$b x^2 + cos(x)")->reduce;
$denom = Compute("$c x^2 + $a")->reduce;

BEGIN_PGML
[` \displaystyle \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{[$numer]}{[$denom]} =  `]  [_]{$b/$c}

END_PGML

$numer1 = Compute("$b x^2 -1")->reduce;
$numer2 = Compute("$b x^2 +1")->reduce;

BEGIN_PGML_HINT

Since [`-1 \le \cos(x) \le 1`], we have 

[``` \frac{[$numer1]}{[$denom]} \leq \frac{[$numer]}{[$denom]} 
\leq \frac{[$numer2]}{[$denom]} ```]

Now apply the squeeze theorem.
END_PGML_HINT

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

Since [`-1 \le \cos(x) \le 1`], we have 
[``` \frac{[$numer1]}{[$denom]} \leq \frac{[$numer]}{[$denom]} 
\leq \frac{[$numer2]}{[$denom]} ```]. 

Let's focus on:


 [``` \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{[$numer1]}{[$denom]} = \lim_{x \to \infty} 
  \frac{[$b]+\frac{1}{x^2}}{[$c]+\frac{2010}{x^2}} = \frac{[$b]}{[$c]}```]

Similarly:


[``` \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{[$numer2]}{[$denom]} = \lim_{x \to \infty} 
  \frac{[$b]-\frac{1}{x^2}}{[$c]+\frac{2010}{x^2}} = \frac{[$b]}{[$c]}```]

 Therefore, by the squeeze theorem: 

[``` \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{[$numer1]}{[$denom]}  = \frac{[$b]}{[$c]}  ```]

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();      

